    "0b119b3d-5f97-5c21-b3db-de60ba1ea50d": {
      "type": "StorySegmentLayer",
      "name": "3. Why Delhi, why November",
      "visible": true,
      "parameters": {
        "zoom": 5.14,
        "extent": [
          2226389.82,
          445640.11,
          11131949.08,
          4579425.81
        ],
        "enableIdentify": true,
        "content": {
          "contentMode": "map",
          "markdown": "<div style='font-size:18px; line-height:1.55; min-height:84vh;'><div style='font-size:14px; color:#64748b; letter-spacing:.03em; margin:0 0 8px;'>0 · Background</div><ul style='margin:0 0 10px 22px; padding:0;'><li style='margin:0 0 5px;'>Delhi-NCR suffers some of the world's worst particulate episodes from late October to January. Stubble burning in Punjab and Haryana, Diwali fireworks and traffic, construction and power emissions coincide with winter's <b>shallow mixing layer (100–300 m) and weak winds</b>, which trap pollution near the ground.</li><li style='margin:0 0 5px;'>India runs a national AQI (CPCB scale) and a staged response plan, <b>GRAP</b> (run by CAQM). AQI bands map to construction bans, vehicle restrictions, online classes and work-from-home, so <b>forecast → stage → action</b> already exists.</li><li style='margin:0 0 5px;'>The question is not \"how bad\" but <b>\"when, where, because of which weather, and what should citizens do\"</b>, 12 to 48 hours ahead.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 10px;'>The square on the map is the Delhi-NCR tile this example covers (27.5–31.5°N, 76–80°E).</p></div>",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "50%"
        },
        "transition": {
          "type": "smooth",
          "time": 2.0
        },
